Visible front gets 1/4 cherry plywood or veneer skin.</pre>
  <p>The structural base is intended to use birch offcuts. CabinetDrafter nests those pieces with the other birch parts when possible.</p>

  <h3>7. Built-in 3D preview</h3>
  <p>The 3D Preview is completely local and does not require SketchUp. It is generated from the same cabinet dimensions used by the BOM and cut list.</p>
  <ul><li>Drag to orbit around the cabinets.</li><li>Use the mouse wheel to zoom.</li><li>Shift-drag to pan.</li><li>Use Front, Left, Right, Top, or Isometric for preset views.</li><li>Turn fronts/doors, drawer boxes, or backs on and off to inspect construction.</li><li>Click Save PNG to save the current view as an image.</li></ul>
  <p>The browser preview uses flat birch/cherry colors and black part outlines. It is intended as a construction/design view, not a photorealistic renderer.</p>

  <h3>8. 3D / SketchUp labels</h3>
  <p>Each label type can be switched on or off independently:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Cabinet summary labels.</li>
    <li>Drawer box heights.</li>
    <li>Drawer face heights.</li>
    <li>Open-space heights for mixed cubbies and open-shelf openings.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Drawer/opening height numbers are placed directly in the visual center of the corresponding face/opening. Inch labels use practical fractions rounded to the nearest 1/16; metric labels use millimeters.</p>

  <h3>9. Saving projects</h3>
  <p>CabinetDrafter uses two layers of local saving:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Browser recovery copy:</strong> every edit is stored in browser local storage.</li>
    <li><strong>Project JSON:</strong> click Save Project JSON once and choose a file. When the browser permits direct file access, CabinetDrafter auto-saves that file after edits and before Ruby export/copy.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Use <strong>Save Project As…</strong> to branch a design. Use <strong>Export JSON copy</strong> for a portable snapshot. Use <strong>Load / Import JSON</strong> to open somebody else's project.</p>

  <h3>10. Sharing CabinetDrafter</h3>
  <p>To give the tool to somebody else, send the CabinetDrafter ZIP. They can unzip it and open <code>index.html</code> directly in a modern browser. To share a specific design, send the project JSON too.</p>

  <h3>11. BOM, face-frame stock, and sheet layouts</h3>
  <p>The sheet optimizer is a planning/nesting aid, not a table-saw cut sequence. It accounts for the configured sheet size, edge trim, saw kerf, and cherry grain direction. Inspect real sheets for damage, voids, and grain before cutting.</p>
  <div class="help-warning"><strong>Current run assumption:</strong> CabinetDrafter treats the complete cabinet list as one installed straight run when it creates the continuous cherry toe-kick skin. If a project contains cabinets on multiple walls or separate runs, split them into separate project files or manually split the toe-kick skin until multi-run support is added.</div>

  <h3>12. Before you cut</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Confirm actual plywood thickness; nominal 3/4 and 1/2 sheet goods may measure less.</li>
    <li>Confirm drawer-slide side clearance before cutting drawer boxes. The default is 1/2 per side.</li>
    <li>Choose the actual concealed hinge before boring door cups or mounting-plate holes.</li>
    <li>Confirm countertop overhangs and wall conditions separately; CabinetDrafter currently models cabinets, not room walls/counters.</li>
    <li>Review every warning in the sizing/BOM section.</li>
    <li>Treat the generated cut list as a verified plan only after checking a representative cabinet against your real hardware/materials.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>13. Current limitations</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>One global cabinet height and depth for the whole project.</li>
    <li>Only single-door cabinets; no paired/double-door cabinet type yet.</li>
    <li>No fillers, scribes, applied finished end panels, corner cabinets, or room/wall layout.</li>
    <li>No hardware BOM yet for slides, hinges, shelf pins, pocket screws, or installation fasteners.</li>
    <li>No automatic hinge cup-bore or shelf-pin drilling templates.</li>
    <li>No explicit multiple cabinet runs/walls; continuous toe-kick skin assumes one run.</li>
    <li>No edge treatment or pull drilling because those choices are still intentionally TBD.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>14. Troubleshooting</h3>
  <p><strong>The SketchUp model did not change:</strong> use the exact timestamped Ruby filename and load command CabinetDrafter shows after downloading. Each generated script removes the old <em>Generated Cabinet Lineup</em> group before rebuilding it.</p>
  <p><strong>Project JSON is not auto-saving:</strong> browsers may require write permission again after reopening a local HTML file. Click Save Project JSON to reconnect the file. Browser recovery storage continues to work independently.</p>
  <p><strong>An open-shelf height list does not fit:</strong> use <code>auto</code> for the top opening, or adjust the entered clear heights so the total plus shelf thicknesses fits the available vertical space.</p>
